{\b\fs22 Before you install NTPods}\par\par
{\b Test Mode required. Not recommended for your everyday or work PC.}\par
Test Mode weakens Windows' normal driver-signature checks. These experimental drivers are not Microsoft production-signed. Use a dedicated test PC.\par\par
This is an experimental development build. Short stereo recordings and twenty capture/stop cycles passed on AirPods Pro 3. Sustained capture with recovery, latency and drift measurements, and broader hardware checks remain pending. See docs/WINDOWS-VALIDATION.md for test results.\par\par
NTPods includes AAP control, a virtual microphone and an AirPods playback driver. The playback driver replaces the output driver for AirPods 1-4, Pro 1-3 and the original Max (Lightning and USB-C). Only Pro 1 and Pro 3 have passed NTPods playback checks. NTPods must remain running to play audio. Uninstall restores the saved playback driver, or Microsoft's driver for pairs first connected after installation. These drivers are not signed by Microsoft, so this installer signs them with a test certificate made on this PC. Windows only loads drivers signed that way in {\b Test Mode}.\par\par
Keep Test Mode on while you use the NTPods drivers. Turning it off, or turning Secure Boot back on, stops the drivers from loading. Setup does not change these boot settings. After uninstalling NTPods, turn Test Mode off and restore any Secure Boot setting you changed.\par\par
{\b BitLocker:} changing Secure Boot or Test Mode can make Windows ask for your BitLocker recovery key at the next boot. If BitLocker is on, make sure that key is saved somewhere other than this PC before you go on (https://aka.ms/myrecoverykey, or in an admin terminal: manage-bde -protectors -get C:).\par\par
The installer puts NTPods in Program Files, installs the drivers, and adds two scheduled tasks that NTPods uses to fix the driver and rename the microphone without asking for admin rights every time. Your settings and heart-rate history stay in %LOCALAPPDATA%\NTPods. Uninstalling removes the drivers, the tasks and the test certificate, and keeps that folder.\par\par
Setup also makes this PC advertise Apple's Bluetooth vendor ID so AirPods expose their supported settings. This applies to all Bluetooth devices. Uninstall restores the original values; upgrades keep them backed up.\par\par
Restart your PC when the installer asks, then connect your AirPods.\par\par
